---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { usd, pct } from "../lib/funding.ts";
import reg from "../data/vesting-contracts.json";

/**
 * Unlocks, built the only way this site is allowed to build it: from schedules read out of
 * the contract, never from a project's published table.
 *
 * Coverage is small on purpose and the page says so in the first paragraph. The register is
 * a data file — adding a token is adding an entry, and nothing here changes.
 */
const now = Date.now();
const d = (s: string) => Date.parse(s + "T00:00:00Z");
const fmt = (s: string) => new Date(d(s)).toLocaleDateString("en-GB", { day: "numeric", month: "short", year: "numeric", timeZone: "UTC" });
const tokenAmount = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString("en-US");

type Sched = { label: string; contract: string; amount: number; begin: string; cliff: string; end: string; lastUpdate: string | null };
interface Row extends Sched { symbol: string; name: string; supply: number; active: boolean; elapsed: number }

const rows: Row[] = reg.tokens.flatMap((t: any) =>
  (t.schedules as Sched[]).map((s) => {
    const b = d(s.begin), e = d(s.end);
    return {
      ...s,
      symbol: t.symbol,
      name: t.name,
      supply: t.totalSupply,
      active: now < e,
      elapsed: Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (now - b) / Math.max(1, e - b))),
    };
  }),
);
const active = rows.filter((r) => r.active);
const complete = rows.filter((r) => !r.active);
const totalVested = rows.reduce((s, r) => s + r.amount, 0);
const contractsRead = rows.length;
const tokensCovered = reg.tokens.length;

const title = "Token unlocks, read from the contract | Basis";
const description =
  "Vesting schedules verified by reading the contract, not copied from a project's published table. Coverage is deliberately small: most tokens have no on-chain schedule to read.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Unlocks</h1>
    <p>
      Vesting schedules read directly out of the contract that holds the tokens. Every figure
      below was returned by an <code>eth_call</code> at block
      {" "}<strong>{reg.verifiedBlock.toLocaleString("en-US")}</strong>, not copied from a
      project's published table. Coverage is <strong>{tokensCovered} tokens</strong> — and the
      reason it is that small is the most useful thing on this page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card card--lead">
      <div class="card__label">Contracts verified</div>
      <div class="card__value">{contractsRead}</div>
      <div class="card__meta">across {tokensCovered} tokens, on {reg.chain}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card__label">Schedules still running</div>
      <div class:list={["card__value", active.length === 0 && "dim"]}>{active.length}</div>
      <div class="card__meta">of {contractsRead} read</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card__label">Tokens under these contracts</div>
      <div class="card__value">{(totalVested / 1e6).toFixed(0)}M</div>
      <div class="card__meta">across every schedule listed</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card__label">Addresses rejected</div>
      <div class="card__value">{reg.unverified.length}</div>
      <div class="card__meta">no readable schedule — listed below</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {active.length === 0 && (
    <div class="verdict verdict--warn">
      <div>
        <b>Not one verified schedule is still running.</b> Every contract we could establish and
        read has finished vesting — the most recent ended {fmt(complete[0] ? complete.reduce((a, b) => (d(a.end) > d(b.end) ? a : b)).end : "2024-09-17")}.
        So this page currently publishes <b>no future unlock dates at all</b>, and that is the
        honest output rather than a gap: an unlock calendar assembled from project announcements
        would have plenty of rows, none of them checkable. When a running schedule is verified,
        it appears here with its next release, its share of supply, and how many days of average
        volume it represents.
      </div>
    </div>
  )}

  {active.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h2 class="section">Running schedules</h2>
      <div class="panel">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Schedule</th><th class="num">Amount</th><th class="num">Share of supply</th><th class="num">Ends</th><th class="num">Elapsed</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {active.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="sym">{r.symbol}</td>
                <td>{r.label}</td>
                <td class="num">{tokenAmount(r.amount)}</td>
                <td class="num">{pct(r.amount / r.supply)}</td>
                <td class="num">{fmt(r.end)}</td>
                <td class="num">{pct(r.elapsed)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 class="section">The verified register</h2>
  <p class="section-note">
    Every row is one contract. The address is the thing to check: read it yourself and you must
    get these numbers back.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Token</th><th>Schedule</th><th class="num">Amount</th><th class="num">Share of supply</th>
          <th class="num">Begins</th><th class="num">Ends</th><th class="num">State</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="sym">{r.symbol}</td>
            <td>{r.label}<br /><code class="addr">{r.contract}</code></td>
            <td class="num">{tokenAmount(r.amount)}</td>
            <td class="num">{pct(r.amount / r.supply)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(r.begin)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(r.end)}</td>
            <td class="num">{r.active ? "running" : "complete"}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  {reg.tokens.map((t: any) => (
    <>
      <h2 class="section">{t.name} — how it was verified</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Contract pattern <strong>{t.pattern}</strong>, source published by the project at
        {" "}<a class="link" href={t.source} rel="nofollow noopener">{t.source.replace("https://", "")}</a>.
        Each address was read with these getters: {t.getters.map((g: string, i: number) => (<><code>{g}</code>{i < t.getters.length - 1 ? ", " : ""}</>))}.
        The token getter had to return
        {" "}<code class="addr">{t.token}</code> — an address that claims to vest a token but points
        somewhere else is not evidence of anything.
      </p>
      <p class="section-note">
        {tokenAmount(t.schedules.reduce((s: number, x: Sched) => s + x.amount, 0))} {t.symbol} passed
        through {t.schedules.length} contract{t.schedules.length === 1 ? "" : "s"} —
        {" "}{pct(t.schedules.reduce((s: number, x: Sched) => s + x.amount, 0) / t.totalSupply)} of a
        {" "}{tokenAmount(t.totalSupply)} total supply, itself read from the token's own
        {" "}<code>totalSupply()</code>.
      </p>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2 class="section">Addresses that did not qualify</h2>
  <p class="section-note">
    Named in project material as allocations, but exposing nothing a schedule can be read from.
    They are listed because the absence is the finding.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Why it gets no row above</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {reg.unverified.map((u: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td style="white-space:nowrap">{u.label}<br /><code class="addr">{u.address}</code></td>
            <td class="reason">{u.reason}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section">Why coverage is this small</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Three walls, in order of how often they stop us. <strong>First</strong>, most tokens have no
    on-chain schedule at all: the allocation sits in a multisig and the release dates live in a
    blog post. There is nothing to read, so there is nothing to verify. <strong>Second</strong>,
    there is no registry mapping a token to its vesting contract, and no ERC standard for vesting
    — every project rolls its own interface, so each one is bespoke work rather than a pipeline.
    <strong> Third</strong>, where a project deploys one escrow per recipient from a factory, the
    individual addresses only appear in factory events, and free RPC endpoints cap
    {" "}<code>eth_getLogs</code> at 50 blocks — roughly ten minutes of chain. The Lido entry above
    is exactly that case: the interface is readable, the addresses are not reachable at this cost.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    The alternative is what every unlock tracker does: publish the project's own numbers. That
    produces a full calendar quickly and it is unverifiable by construction — when a date slips or
    an allocation is quietly restructured, nothing in the data says so. A short list you can check
    is worth more than a long one you cannot.
    <a class="link" href="/data-sources">Where the rest of the site's data comes from →</a>
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">What would grow this</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    An archival RPC endpoint with unrestricted <code>eth_getLogs</code> would unlock every
    factory-deployed escrow in one pass — that is the single highest-yield addition. Beyond that,
    coverage grows one contract at a time, and each addition is an entry in a data file rather
    than a change to this page.
  </p>

  <style>
    .addr { font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--text-faint); background: none; padding: 0; }
    .reason { color: var(--text-dim); white-space: normal; }
    .tbl td { vertical-align: top; }
    h2.section { margin-top: 32px; }
  </style>
</Base>
